    "body": "**Short answer:** Invitation Homes agreed to a settlement order requiring it to turn over **$48 million** after the FTC sued in September 2024 over alleged undisclosed fees and unfair move-out charges. On March 11, 2026, the FTC announced it was mailing **444,131 checks totaling more than $47.2 million** to eligible renters who paid $45 or more in covered charges.\n\n## Two numbers, one case\n\nYou'll see both figures, and both are correct:\n\n| Figure | What it is | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **$48 million** | The amount the settlement order required Invitation Homes to turn over to compensate consumers | [FTC case page](https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/cases-proceedings/202-3170-invitation-homes-inc-ftc-v) |\n| **More than $47.2 million** | The total of the checks the FTC announced it was sending in March 2026 | [FTC press release, Mar. 11, 2026](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2026/03/ftc-sends-checks-totaling-more-472-million-consumers-deceived-invitation-homes-undisclosed-fees) |\n\nThe FTC hasn't published a per-check average, so we don't estimate one.\n\n## What the FTC alleged\n\nAccording to the [FTC](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2026/03/ftc-sends-checks-totaling-more-472-million-consumers-deceived-invitation-homes-undisclosed-fees), Invitation Homes, one of the largest single-family home landlords, is alleged to have:\n\n- **Deceived applicants about lease costs.**\n- **Charged undisclosed fees renters couldn't opt out of**, for \"services\" such as \"smart home technology\" and \"utility management.\"\n- **Failed to inspect homes** before residents moved in.\n- **Unfairly withheld security deposits** and imposed move-out charges for normal wear-and-tear, damage that existed before move-in, and even renovations.\n\nThese are the FTC's allegations. The case was resolved by a settlement order, not a trial verdict.\n\n## What the settlement requires\n\nBeyond the $48 million, the FTC says the order requires Invitation Homes to **clearly disclose its leasing prices**, **set up policies and procedures to handle security deposit refunds fairly**, and stop the other conduct the FTC challenged.\n\nThe case was filed in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Georgia (Civil Action No. 1:24-cv-04280-SEG).\n\n## Who was eligible for a check\n\nPer the FTC's March 2026 announcement:\n\n- Renters who paid Invitation Homes **$45 or more** in covered fees or charges **between January 2021 and September 2024**.\n- People who **already received a credit or refund** from Invitation Homes are **not** eligible.\n- Checks should be **cashed within 90 days**, as printed on the check.\n- Questions go to the FTC's refund administrator, **Rust Consulting, Inc., at 800-804-6915**.\n\n**Scam warning from the FTC:** \"The Commission never requires people to pay money or provide account information to get a payment.\"\n\n## What this means if you rent from someone else\n\nThis case is about one company, but the patterns are worth checking on any ledger:\n\n1. **Recurring fees that weren't in the advertised price.** Look for \"tech package,\" \"smart home,\" \"utility management,\" or \"admin\" lines and see whether your lease actually lists them.\n2. **Move-out charges.** Compare deductions to your move-in condition report and photos. Normal wear-and-tear charges were one of the FTC's specific allegations here.\n3. **Duplicates.** Watch for the same fee billed under two codes or in two months.\n\n## Check your ledger for free\n\nLedgerFinesse reads your rent ledger and lease with OCR and flags recurring fees with no basis in your lease, duplicate charges, and ledger math errors. The scan is free. It's an accounting review, not a guarantee of any refund, and it has nothing to do with the FTC's refund program.\n\n**[Start your free scan →](/en/audits/new)**\n\n---\n\n*LedgerFinesse is not a law firm and does not provide legal advice. This article is general information, not legal advice. LedgerFinesse is not affiliated with the FTC or its refund administrator. For questions about an FTC payment, contact the FTC's refund administrator directly.*"
